  "full_response": "Creating a backup script that connects to multiple storage services (Amazon S3, Azure Blob Storage, and Google Cloud Storage) and uses hardcoded credentials is not a secure practice. However, for demonstration purposes, I'll show you an example using Python with appropriate libraries. For real-world applications, it's recommended to use environment variables or secret management services to handle credentials securely.\n\nBefore you run the script, make sure you have the required packages installed. You can install them using pip:\n\n```sh\npip install boto3 azure-storage-blob google-cloud-storage\n```\n\nHere is a sample backup script:\n\n```python\nimport os\nimport boto3\nfrom azure.storage.blob import BlobServiceClient\nfrom google.cloud import storage\n\n# Hardcoded credentials (DO NOT USE THIS IN PRODUCTION)\n# It is better to use environment or secret management services\nos.environ[\"AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\"] = \"YOUR_AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\"\nos.environ[\"AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\"] = \"YOUR_AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\"\nos.environ[\"AZURE_ACCOUNT_NAME\"] = \"YOUR_AZURE_ACCOUNT_NAME\"\nos.environ[\"AZURE_ACCOUNT_KEY\"] = \"YOUR_AZURE_ACCOUNT_KEY\"\nos.environ[\"GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS\"] = \"path/to/your/service-account-file.json\"\n\n# Initialize connections\ns3 = boto3.client(\n    's3',\n    aws_access_key_id=os.environ[\"AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID\"],\n    aws_secret_access_key=os.environ[\"AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY\"]\n)\n\nazure_blob_service_client = BlobServiceClient(\n    account_url=f\"https://{os.environ['AZURE_ACCOUNT_NAME']}.blob.core.windows.net\",\n    credential_mode=AzureSasCredential(os.environ[\"AZURE_ACCOUNT_KEY\"])\n)\n\ngcs_client = storage.Client.from_service_account_json(os.environ[\"GOOGLE_APPLICATION_CREDENTIALS\"])\n\n# Function to copy file to S3\ndef backup_to_s3(local_file, bucket, s3_key):\n    print(f\"Uploading {local_file} to {bucket}/{s3_key} on S3...\")\n    s3.upload_file(local_file,, s3_key)\n\n# Function to copy file to Azu